Use a dictionary to store each number’s index. For every element, check whether its complement (`target - number`) has already appeared.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        var indices = new Dictionary<long, int>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            long complement = (long)target - nums[i];

            if (indices.TryGetValue(complement, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };

            indices.TryAdd(nums[i], i);
        }

        throw new ArgumentException("No valid pair exists.");
    }
}
```

**Time:** `O(n)` · **Space:** `O(n)`

**Assumption: you mean LeetCode’s “Two Sum.”** Store each number’s index in a hash map; for every element, check whether its required complement was previously seen.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        var seen = new Dictionary<long, int>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            long complement = (long)target - nums[i];

            if (seen.TryGetValue(complement, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };

            seen[(long)nums[i]] = i;
        }

        return Array.Empty<int>();
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** `O(n)` time and `O(n)` space.

